WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.855
Welcome to Module Seven: NVIDIA Platform Implementation.

2
00:00:04.185 --> 00:00:07.357
It's about knowing which piece of NVIDIA's stack does which job.

3
00:00:08.157 --> 00:00:11.904
Picture a bank's platform team with a promising prototype assistant.

4
00:00:12.234 --> 00:00:15.832
Under the hood, it's glued together from whatever libraries were handy.

5
00:00:16.632 --> 00:00:20.401
First, customer data can never leave the bank's own infrastructure.

6
00:00:20.731 --> 00:00:24.691
So the model runs on a home-made server that crawls under real traffic.

7
00:00:25.491 --> 00:00:27.148
Second, there are no guardrails.

8
00:00:27.478 --> 00:00:31.631
A tester talks the assistant into ignoring its rules, and nothing stops it.

9
00:00:32.431 --> 00:00:34.407
Third, there's no evaluation gate.

10
00:00:34.737 --> 00:00:38.634
When a fine-tuned model arrives, nobody can say if it beats the old one.

11
00:00:39.434 --> 00:00:41.347
So the team stops adding libraries.

12
00:00:41.677 --> 00:00:45.808
Instead, they map each need to one component with exactly one job.

13
00:00:46.608 --> 00:00:49.136
For serving, they pick NIM: a prebuilt,

14
00:00:49.186 --> 00:00:53.299
GPU-optimised model container running in their own data centre.

15
00:00:53.629 --> 00:00:58.443
Its OpenAI-compatible API means the agent code barely changes.

16
00:00:59.243 --> 00:01:02.244
NeMo Guardrails applies safety rails to live traffic.

17
00:01:02.574 --> 00:01:07.153
NeMo Evaluator becomes the gate: a new model ships only if it scores better.

18
00:01:07.953 --> 00:01:12.085
NeMo Retriever handles document extraction, embedding, and reranking.

19
00:01:12.415 --> 00:01:17.071
The NeMo Agent Toolkit wraps their LangGraph agent with profiling and tracing,

20
00:01:17.121 --> 00:01:18.338
without replacing it.

21
00:01:19.138 --> 00:01:23.931
It all runs on Kubernetes, with the NIM Operator managing the model services.

22
00:01:24.261 --> 00:01:28.072
The glued-together prototype becomes a production reference architecture.

23
00:01:28.872 --> 00:01:31.297
Lesson one maps the stack, layer by layer.

24
00:01:31.627 --> 00:01:34.156
You'll learn each component's one-line job,

25
00:01:34.206 --> 00:01:37.635
like Curator preparing data and Customizer training on it.

26
00:01:38.435 --> 00:01:40.678
It untangles pairs people confuse:

27
00:01:40.728 --> 00:01:44.657
Evaluator measures offline, Guardrails enforce policy live.

28
00:01:44.987 --> 00:01:50.207
NIM is a packaged model service; Triton is a general server you configure yourself.

29
00:01:51.007 --> 00:01:52.927
You'll see why NIM is fast:

30
00:01:52.977 --> 00:01:58.765
its TensorRT-LLM engine batches requests on the fly and runs in lower precision.

31
00:01:59.095 --> 00:02:01.967
And when Dynamo spreads inference across many machines.

32
00:02:02.767 --> 00:02:05.981
Lesson two assembles a full reference architecture,

33
00:02:06.031 --> 00:02:09.757
with Curator, Customizer, and Evaluator in an offline loop.

34
00:02:10.087 --> 00:02:13.920
It also covers sizing GPUs and fully air-gapped deployments.

35
00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:17.139
This domain is seven percent of the exam,

36
00:02:17.189 --> 00:02:20.089
mostly asking which component fits a requirement.

37
00:02:20.419 --> 00:02:22.054
Lesson one starts with the map.
